{"tool_name":"f5c","contents":["biocontainers","bioschemas","biotools","bioconda","oeb"],"fetched_metadata":{"bioconda":{"name":"f5c","version":"1.7","home":"https://github.com/hasindu2008/f5c","documentation":"https://hasindu2008.github.io/f5c/docs/overview","license":"MIT","summary":"An optimised re-implementation of the call-methylation and eventalign modules in Nanopolish.","identifiers":["doi:10.1101/756122","doi:10.1186/s12859-020-03697-x","biotools:f5c"],"license_file":"LICENSE","build":{"number":0,"run_exports":[null]},"requirements":{"build":["make",null,null,null],"host":["zlib","hdf5","wget"],"run":["hdf5"]},"source_url":"https://github.com/hasindu2008/f5c/releases/download/v1.7/f5c-v1.7-release.tar.gz","test":{"commands":["f5c --help","f5c --version"]}},"biocontainers":{"name":"f5c","identifiers":["biotools:f5c","doi:10.1101/756122","doi:10.1186/s12859-020-03697-x"],"license":"MIT","summary":"An optimised re-implementation of the call-methylation and eventalign modules in Nanopolish.","home_url":"https://github.com/hasindu2008/f5c","keywords":["Computational Biology","Computer Graphics","Databases as Topic","Genome, Human","Humans","Nanopores","Sequence Analysis","Signal Processing, Computer-Assisted","Computer science","Epigenetics","Whole genome sequencing"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":1680},"biotools":{"id":"f5c","home":"https://hasindu2008.github.io/f5c/","license":"MIT","summary":"GPU Accelerated Adaptive Banded Event Alignment for Rapid Comparative Nanopore Signal Analysis | Re-engineered and optimised Nanopolish call-methylation module (supports CUDA acceleration) | An optimised re-implementation of the call-methylation module in Nanopolish. Given a set of basecalled Nanopore reads and the raw signals, f5c detects the methylated cytosine bases. f5c can optionally utilise NVIDIA graphics cards for acceleration","addition_date":"2019-11-14T19:30:35Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T20:52:06.081320Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:f5c","documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://hasindu2008.github.io/f5c/docs/overview"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"public"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Base-calling","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3185"},{"term":"Genotyping","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3196"},{"term":"Local alignment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0495"}]}],"name":"f5c","owner":"Pub2Tools","publication":[{"doi":"10.1101/756122"},{"doi":"10.1186/s12859-020-03697-x","metadata":{"abstract":"Background: Nanopore sequencing enables portable, real-time sequencing applications, including point-of-care diagnostics and in-the-field genotyping. Achieving these outcomes requires efficient bioinformatic algorithms for the analysis of raw nanopore signal data. However, comparing raw nanopore signals to a biological reference sequence is a computationally complex task. The dynamic programming algorithm called Adaptive Banded Event Alignment (ABEA) is a crucial step in polishing sequencing data and identifying non-standard nucleotides, such as measuring DNA methylation. Here, we parallelise and optimise an implementation of the ABEA algorithm (termed f5c) to efficiently run on heterogeneous CPU-GPU architectures. Results: By optimising memory, computations and load balancing between CPU and GPU, we demonstrate how f5c can perform \u223c3-5 \u00d7 faster than an optimised version of the original CPU-only implementation of ABEA in the Nanopolish software package. We also show that f5c enables DNA methylation detection on-the-fly using an embedded System on Chip (SoC) equipped with GPUs. Conclusions: Our work not only demonstrates that complex genomics analyses can be performed on lightweight computing systems, but also benefits High-Performance Computing (HPC). The associated source code for f5c along with GPU optimised ABEA is available at https://github.com/hasindu2008/f5c.","authors":[{"name":"Gamaarachchi H."},{"name":"Jayatilaka G."},{"name":"Lam C.W."},{"name":"Parameswaran S."},{"name":"Samarakoon H."},{"name":"Simpson J.T."},{"name":"Smith M.A."}],"citationCount":53,"date":"2020-08-05T00:00:00Z","journal":"BMC Bioinformatics","title":"GPU accelerated adaptive banded event alignment for rapid comparative nanopore signal analysis"},"pmcid":"PMC7430849","pmid":"32758139"}],"topic":[{"term":"Computer science","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3316"},{"term":"Epigenetics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3295"},{"term":"Whole genome sequencing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3673"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"f5c","home":"https://bio.tools/f5c","license":"MIT","summary":"GPU Accelerated Adaptive Banded Event Alignment for Rapid Comparative Nanopore Signal Analysis | Re-engineered and optimised Nanopolish call-methylation module (supports CUDA acceleration) | An optimised re-implementation of the call-methylation module in Nanopolish. Given a set of basecalled Nanopore reads and the raw signals, f5c detects the methylated cytosine bases. f5c can optionally utilise NVIDIA graphics cards for acceleration","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:f5c/cmd/hasindu2008.github.io","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":null,"registration":null},"packages":null,"source_packages":false,"sourcecode":{"free":null,"interpreted":false,"license":null,"public":true,"readme":false,"repository":{"anonymous":null,"online":null}}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"general":true},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":74,"citations":[{"count":4,"year":"2021"},{"count":7,"year":"2022"},{"count":11,"year":"2026"},{"count":12,"year":"2023"},{"count":19,"year":"2025"},{"count":21,"year":"2024"}],"doi":"10.1186/s12859-020-03697-x","pmcid":"PMC7430849","pmid":